WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.120
Previously on... Film Theory!

00:00:02.120 --> 00:00:03.680
Hey, I’ve seen a boat! *echoed*

00:00:03.700 --> 00:00:07.720
How could Dory remember the boat, but then forget meeting Marlin?

00:00:08.600 --> 00:00:11.080
I am on to you, Ellen DeGeneres!

00:00:11.080 --> 00:00:12.860
-Keep swimming, swimming, swimming-

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:16.340
“Finding Dory”? More like “Exposing Dory”!

00:00:16.340 --> 00:00:18.140
The truth is comin’ out!

00:00:18.140 --> 00:00:23.020
Dory doesn't suffer from short term memory loss - she’s faking it.

00:00:23.020 --> 00:00:23.660
/Dramatic thunder clap/

00:00:23.660 --> 00:00:39.580
*Film Theory Theme Song*

00:00:39.580 --> 00:00:44.800
Hello, internet! Welcome to Film- 
wait, what was I saying?

00:00:45.080 --> 00:00:49.560
See, if I had anterograde amnesia like Dory claims, I wouldn't remember what I’m supposed to say here.

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:53.840
But as we talked about last time, Dory
doesn’t have anterograde amnesia.

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:58.440
She’s faking her condition to look weaker and
have the other fish underestimate her abilities.

00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:00.160
It's a survival tactic.

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:04.180
Oh, sure, everything seems to check out.
She can't remember Marlin's name,

00:01:04.180 --> 00:01:07.500
she forgets where she is, can’t even
remember what happened to her parents.

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:10.540
But as we found last episode, she slips up.

00:01:10.540 --> 00:01:15.900
She remembers seeing the boat that took Nemo,
even after she claims to forget who Marlin is.

00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:19.280
This fact of seeing the boat
should fall under declarative memory,

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:23.620
which, if she was truly suffering from short-term memory loss, she should have forgotten.

00:01:23.620 --> 00:01:25.800
You've painted yourself into a corner, Dory.

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:28.140
And that’s not the only piece of
evidence I have against you.

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:36.900
Today we’re throwing the book at Dory to show that appearances can be deceiving and to get you to view Finding Dory in a whole new context.

00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:41.680
And here’s the crazy thing. To start exposing Dory's lies, we don’t even need to look that deep.

00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:46.760
It all begins with the single most basic fact about Dory: the type of fish she is.

00:01:46.760 --> 00:01:53.660
Now, Finding Nemo never specifies her species, but judging by her blue body and the black design running out to her yellow tail,

00:01:53.660 --> 00:01:56.620
we can confidently say that Dory is a blue tang -

00:01:56.620 --> 00:02:01.240
also known as a Paracanthurus Hepatus, Latin for "incredibly hard to pronounce".

00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:05.700
These fish tend to be relatively small, growing up to only twelve inches when they reach adult-hood.

00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:07.720
And look at that peaceful majesty...

00:02:11.780 --> 00:02:17.660
And yet, it's time to explore some true facts about the blue tang.

00:02:17.660 --> 00:02:24.960
That curvy blue body hides a caudal spine, which extends out when threatened, slashing and wounding predators.

00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:28.100
That makes Dory like the Wolverine of fish.

00:02:29.860 --> 00:02:35.020
And that's not all. If consumed by humans, blue tangs may cause poisoning.

00:02:35.020 --> 00:02:39.640
And some blue tang fish even have venomous glands in their fins.

00:02:39.640 --> 00:02:44.480
Wow Dory is far less cute than Finding Nemo would have you believe!

00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:50.780
Behind that angelic blue body and freckled big - eyed face lies a venomous monster...

00:02:50.780 --> 00:02:52.740
ZeFrank I miss you and your true facts series

00:02:52.780 --> 00:02:54.920
Come back to YouTube, COME BACK!!

00:02:54.920 --> 00:02:57.260
But okay, let's go back to the whole memory loss thing.

00:02:57.260 --> 00:03:00.280
Dory states that the source of her short term memory loss is hereditary.

00:03:00.440 --> 00:03:02.960
DORY: I forget things almost instantly it runs in my family

00:03:02.980 --> 00:03:05.800
This may be a simple reference to  the old myth of goldfish memory:

00:03:05.820 --> 00:03:08.540
the belief that fish are dumb and have short memory spans;

00:03:08.540 --> 00:03:11.860
only able to recall events for about three seconds or so.

00:03:11.860 --> 00:03:13.960
This though is... just not true.

00:03:14.360 --> 00:03:15.940
Not A True Fact

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:17.600
about the Blue Tang

00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:20.060
Fish actually have surprisingly strong memories

00:03:20.060 --> 00:03:21.720
lasting for up to a year!

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:24.040
Carp that have been caught by fishermen have been seen

00:03:24.100 --> 00:03:26.500
to avoid hooks for up to a year afterwards

00:03:26.500 --> 00:03:29.000
Researchers even discovered that they can learn to associate

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:32.720
certain sounds with food and then remember it 5 months later!

00:03:32.720 --> 00:03:36.540
There is ample evidence that fish are just as intelligent as mammals,

00:03:36.540 --> 00:03:39.900
capable of the same intellectual feats as rats or mice.

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:43.360
They can learn their way around mazes; they can learn to recognise other fish

00:03:43.360 --> 00:03:45.660
and they can remember prey from predator.

00:03:45.780 --> 00:03:50.920
Thus we can't dismiss Dory's short term memory loss as just being symptomatic of her being a fish

00:03:50.920 --> 00:03:55.220
So the question remains then: Can you inherit short term memory loss from your parents?

00:03:55.220 --> 00:03:58.360
The answer here is, once again, is a big NO.

00:03:58.360 --> 00:04:01.480
Short-term memory loss is typically caused in one of two ways:

00:04:01.480 --> 00:04:04.460
drug and alcohol abuse or severe brain trauma.

00:04:04.460 --> 00:04:10.560
Benzodiazepine drugs, typically used to treat panic attacks, if misused can cause amnesia induced side effects;

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:14.540
drinking a bunch of alcohol can cause blackouts which blocks the brain's ability

00:04:14.540 --> 00:04:17.760
to transfer short-term memories into long term memory.

00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:20.960
So if you go out the late night bender and can't remember what happened to you the night before,

00:04:20.960 --> 00:04:24.560
congratulations! You just gave yourself anterograde amnesia!

00:04:24.560 --> 00:04:26.560
A winner is YOU!

00:04:26.560 --> 00:04:29.140
And chronic alcoholism can lead to Korsakoff's Syndrome,

00:04:29.180 --> 00:04:31.680
where brain cells aren't able to produce enough energy

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:35.240
and as a result break down and cause permanent anterograde amnesia.

00:04:35.240 --> 00:04:38.980
So unless Dory is regularly swimming her way out of a bourbon bottle,

00:04:38.980 --> 00:04:40.040
it's not gonna do it.

00:04:40.040 --> 00:04:42.340
Although, That would be a very different movie

00:04:42.360 --> 00:04:43.880
* Dramatic music begins * New from Pixar,

00:04:43.880 --> 00:04:48.920
An uplifting family friendly story of one fish's struggle to combat alcoholism

00:04:48.920 --> 00:04:52.280
Coming this summer, Finding Dory - In The Gutter

00:04:52.280 --> 00:04:54.460
*Just keep swimming, slowed down A LOT*

00:04:54.460 --> 00:04:55.180
Rated R

00:04:55.180 --> 00:04:58.420
On the other side traumatic brain injury, typically to the hippocampus

00:04:58.420 --> 00:05:01.640
and surrounding cortices can also cause anterograde amnesia.

00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:06.580
Or to treat a serious disorder like epilepsy or a tumor, patients will have parts of their brain removed.

00:05:06.580 --> 00:05:11.000
These procedures successfully cure the disorder, but can often resolve in anterograde amnesia.

00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:15.980
Notice though that none of these instances, Brain Trauma, Alcohol &amp; Drug Abuse are hereditary.

00:05:15.980 --> 00:05:22.500
In fact, there is no scientific proof that anterograde amnesia has ever been passed down genetically.

00:05:22.500 --> 00:05:23.320
NONE!

00:05:23.340 --> 00:05:28.580
Dory, unless she is a unicorn of biology quite simply is lying about the cause of her amnesia.

00:05:28.580 --> 00:05:33.180
Which again, throws into question her having amnesia in the first place!

00:05:33.180 --> 00:05:37.460
But that sounds crazy right, people faking an illness as extreme as amnesia!

00:05:37.460 --> 00:05:40.140
But here's the thing, it's not as crazy as it sounds.

00:05:40.140 --> 00:05:42.140
In fact it's so common that it even has a name.

00:05:42.140 --> 00:05:45.340
Faking amnesia would be considered a type of 'Malingering',

00:05:45.340 --> 00:05:48.860
in which people fake symptoms of an illness to achieve some goal.

00:05:48.860 --> 00:05:54.900
Getting out of work,  getting taken care of or in Dory's case, increasing your odds of survival in the dangerous ocean.

00:05:54.900 --> 00:05:58.020
So, outside of the scientific inconsistencies in her story,

00:05:58.020 --> 00:06:01.440
How do we prove, outright PROVE, that she's malingering?

00:06:01.440 --> 00:06:03.260
Well, it's not as hard as you might think.

00:06:03.260 --> 00:06:06.120
Most fakers tend to Ferris Bueller their symptoms,

00:06:06.120 --> 00:06:10.180
over-exaggerate them by putting in extra effort to make them look sick.

00:06:10.180 --> 00:06:13.000
FERRIS: "The key to faking out the parents is the clammy hands."

00:06:13.000 --> 00:06:15.000
Take for instance the "hand coin" test.

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:17.040
If I put a quarter in my RIGHT hand,

00:06:17.040 --> 00:06:19.440
you can easily tell me which hand the coin is in.

00:06:19.440 --> 00:06:25.360
Unless of course, you WEREN'T PAYING ATTENTION, XxPixarLover15! [Pissed MatPat is PISSED]

00:06:25.360 --> 00:06:29.360
There's ALWAYS one of you; jeez, you think I do these videos for my health or something?

00:06:29.360 --> 00:06:31.000
I'm doing this for YOU. [MatPat please calm yourself]

00:06:31.000 --> 00:06:34.680
Anyway, to CATCH YOU UP, the coin is in my right hand.

00:06:34.680 --> 00:06:38.760
Amnesiacs, though, can't remember which hand
the coin is in and are forced to guess.

00:06:38.760 --> 00:06:43.460
Meaning that, by the laws of probability, they'll guess correctly around fifty percent of the time.

00:06:43.460 --> 00:06:47.200
Fakers, though, will exaggerate their bad performance.

00:06:47.200 --> 00:06:52.140
They'll remember which hand the coin is in and to look sick, will deliberately choose the wrong one.

00:06:52.140 --> 00:06:57.200
As a result, their tally of correct to incorrect guesses
will be below chance.

00:06:57.200 --> 00:07:00.060
They're throwing the test, but
they're throwing it a bit too hard!

00:07:00.060 --> 00:07:04.880
And when it comes to short-term memory loss, there's a BUNCH of proven scientific tests to catch you in the act.

00:07:04.880 --> 00:07:07.720
These tests are referred to as symptom validity tests.

00:07:07.720 --> 00:07:11.420
For example, the test of memory malingering,
otherwise known as TOMM,

00:07:11.420 --> 00:07:15.660
is a 50-question visual recognition test,
where examinees are shown fifty drawings,

00:07:15.660 --> 00:07:18.880
numbers, words or symbols for
around three seconds each.

00:07:18.880 --> 00:07:23.320
After all the drawings have been shown, the examiner then shows the examinees two different drawings:

00:07:23.320 --> 00:07:26.380
one from the fifty previously shown and one new one.

00:07:26.380 --> 00:07:30.140
The examinee is then asked to name which of the two drawings he had previously seen.

00:07:30.140 --> 00:07:33.440
By purely guessing, an amnesiac
would score fifty percent right.

00:07:33.440 --> 00:07:37.620
And across all of these tests,
it was found that suspected malingerers

00:07:37.620 --> 00:07:41.440
scored worse than actual anterograde amnesiacs.

00:07:41.440 --> 00:07:42.220
And that's not all:

00:07:42.220 --> 00:07:45.200
malingerers tend to have
a slower response time to questions.

00:07:45.200 --> 00:07:47.060
They take great pains in answering,

00:07:47.060 --> 00:07:50.000
as if they're struggling to figure out
what the correct answer was.

00:07:50.000 --> 00:07:53.420
(MatPat mocking) hmmmm, uhhhhh,
oooooh, that's a tough one, erghhhh.

00:07:53.420 --> 00:07:57.880
True amnesiacs, however, tend not to have that much delay between question and answer.

00:07:57.880 --> 00:08:02.600
And finally, psychologists used what's known as
the primacy effect to test so-called amnesiacs.

00:08:02.620 --> 00:08:07.180
This is the tendency to more easily remember items from the beginning of a list rather than the middle.

00:08:07.180 --> 00:08:08.300
So if I give you the numbers

00:08:08.300 --> 00:08:11.760
8, 2, 9, 6, 3, 5, 5, 9, 6, 2,

00:08:11.760 --> 00:08:13.920
you're most likely to remember the number 8,

00:08:13.920 --> 00:08:16.040
because it was the first number of the bunch.

00:08:16.040 --> 00:08:19.480
Fakers show this effect, people with amnesia don't.

00:08:19.480 --> 00:08:21.560
Knowing all that, look at Dory.

00:08:21.560 --> 00:08:27.100
Dory, like most malingerers, consistently answers questions wrong, far below mere chance.

00:08:27.100 --> 00:08:30.400
Midway through Finding Nemo, a school
of fish tries to give Dory directions by

00:08:30.400 --> 00:08:32.660
showing her a number of different sea creatures.

00:08:32.660 --> 00:08:35.380
However, Dory is unable to identify a single one,

00:08:35.380 --> 00:08:39.220
failing to recognize a swordfish,
a lobster and an octopus.

00:08:39.220 --> 00:08:40.640
Now, you're probably thinking:

00:08:40.640 --> 00:08:42.420
Hey MatPat, give Dory a break.

00:08:42.420 --> 00:08:45.360
Maybe she can't remember what
an octopus or swordfish are called.

00:08:45.360 --> 00:08:47.860
She does have anterograde amnesia, after all.

00:08:47.860 --> 00:08:53.460
Except, Dory previously has shown that she has quite a knack for remembering different aquatic life.

00:08:53.460 --> 00:08:55.260
She easily identifies a shark, -

00:08:55.260 --> 00:08:56.400
DORY: "Hey look, shark!"

00:08:56.400 --> 00:08:57.160
- a whale, -

00:08:57.180 --> 00:08:58.820
DORY: "O-oh, big fella! Whale!"

00:08:58.820 --> 00:08:59.800
- even a seagull!

00:08:59.800 --> 00:09:02.660
/Dory glares at the crab and holds it up for the seagulls to see./

00:09:02.660 --> 00:09:03.180
SEAGULL: "Mine."

00:09:03.180 --> 00:09:08.540
So how come right then and there, she's unable to
come up with three very common sea creatures?

00:09:08.540 --> 00:09:12.260
Sure, maybe she can't name one or two, but... all three?

00:09:12.260 --> 00:09:14.660
It's almost like she's deliberately getting them wrong:

00:09:14.660 --> 00:09:18.860
playing into her short-term memory loss,
answering way below chance.

00:09:18.860 --> 00:09:21.740
And in that scene, Dory agonizes
over her answers: [Roll tape]

00:09:21.740 --> 00:09:22.600
DORY: "Wait, um-"

00:09:22.600 --> 00:09:23.480
MARLIN: (impatiently) "It's a swordfish!"

00:09:23.480 --> 00:09:24.960
MOONFISH: "Where's the butter?"

00:09:24.960 --> 00:09:26.620
DORY: "Oh-oh-oh! It's on the tip of my tongue."

00:09:26.620 --> 00:09:30.540
Much like a malingerer would,
she exaggerates her condition:

00:09:30.540 --> 00:09:32.800
a telltale sign of a FAKER.

00:09:32.800 --> 00:09:36.840
You want more proof? Look no further than
all the times Dory gets Nemo's name wrong.

00:09:36.840 --> 00:09:40.620
She calls him Fabio, Chico, Bingo, Harpo, Elmo...

00:09:40.620 --> 00:09:45.140
Now the fact that Dory can't remember Nemo's name is consistent with anterograde amnesia,

00:09:45.140 --> 00:09:47.380
but look closely at all the names she calls him:

00:09:47.380 --> 00:09:50.520
they all end with an O, just like Nemo.

00:09:50.520 --> 00:09:53.600
But someone really suffering from anterograde amnesia

00:09:53.600 --> 00:09:56.760
would be just as likely to call Nemo...
John [OMG what did you do to that poor fish],

00:09:56.760 --> 00:09:58.660
or Paul [STAHP], or Ringo [JFC].

00:09:58.660 --> 00:10:02.220
They would have no idea, no context,
that his name would end with an O.

00:10:02.220 --> 00:10:07.300
The very fact that Dory chooses names
that are close to Nemo, but not quite,

00:10:07.300 --> 00:10:12.800
is proof that she remembers his name and is deliberately struggling and trying to get them wrong.

00:10:12.800 --> 00:10:16.220
Dory even recalls information in
accordance with the primacy effect:

00:10:16.220 --> 00:10:18.920
she memorizes the address for
where Nemo is being held -

00:10:18.920 --> 00:10:21.000
P. Sherman
42 Wallaby Way
Sydney

00:10:21.000 --> 00:10:23.640
- yet in her moving speech
to Marlin at the end of the movie,

00:10:23.640 --> 00:10:28.420
she struggles to remember the complete address, 
only able to identify the beginning -

00:10:28.420 --> 00:10:30.420
just like a faker would.

00:10:30.420 --> 00:10:36.700
DORY: "P. Sherman, 42- f- ugh... forty... two... ugh!"

00:10:36.980 --> 00:10:41.940
In fact, throughout Finding Nemo,
Dory shows inconsistent base knowledge:

00:10:41.940 --> 00:10:44.520
when she meets Bruce the shark, she's not scared at all,

00:10:44.520 --> 00:10:47.640
like she doesn't know what sharks are,
or that they eat fish!

00:10:47.640 --> 00:10:50.240
Yet later, after discovering a scuba mask,

00:10:50.240 --> 00:10:53.080
she correctly identifies sharks below her.

00:10:53.080 --> 00:10:54.000
DORY: "Hey look, shark!"

00:10:54.000 --> 00:10:54.640
MARLIN: "No no no-!"

00:10:54.680 --> 00:10:59.400
And then one more time, by the end of the film,
she tells the sharks to remember not to eat fish.

00:10:59.840 --> 00:11:01.720
ANCHOR: "Remember, fish are friends..."

00:11:01.720 --> 00:11:03.800
DORY: "...not food! Bye!"

00:11:03.800 --> 00:11:07.540
So which way is it, Dory? Do you remember what a shark is or NOT?

00:11:07.540 --> 00:11:09.620
Do you remember that they eat fish or NOT?

00:11:09.620 --> 00:11:12.840
'Cuz if you didn't know that they eat fish the first time you met 'em,

00:11:12.840 --> 00:11:18.020
then you shouldn't remember hours and days later
at the end of the movie when you see them again.

00:11:18.020 --> 00:11:22.340
So which is it, Dory?
Do you know whether sharks eat fish or NOT?

00:11:22.340 --> 00:11:23.820
CHECKMATE!!

00:11:23.820 --> 00:11:27.560
Checkmate, you fictional, computer-animated fish, you!!

00:11:27.560 --> 00:11:29.300
WE GOTCHA!!!
[MatPat calm down will you please--]

00:11:29.300 --> 00:11:33.180
So you always thought Finding Nemo was
a wholesome tale about a good-hearted father

00:11:33.180 --> 00:11:37.400
finding his kidnapped son with the help of
a lovable but simple-minded companion,

00:11:37.400 --> 00:11:38.160
NOPE.

00:11:38.160 --> 00:11:40.460
Let me break down what's really happening here.

00:11:40.460 --> 00:11:45.440
Dory, completely alone, sees another lone fish, screaming about his missing child;

00:11:45.440 --> 00:11:47.940
a kindred spirit, an easy mark.

00:11:47.940 --> 00:11:53.440
Dory uses her perceived weakness to trick predators,
like the shark, whale, jellyfish, anglerfish,

00:11:53.440 --> 00:11:55.660
who all just buy what Dory's selling.

00:11:55.660 --> 00:11:59.080
When Marlin attempts to get away from her,
Dory immediately begins to cry,

00:11:59.080 --> 00:12:02.260
emotionally manipulating the clownfish
to take her along with him.

00:12:02.260 --> 00:12:05.220
Over the course of the film,
she pretends to slowly get better,

00:12:05.220 --> 00:12:07.400
linking her perceived progress with Marlin.

00:12:07.400 --> 00:12:12.180
Now, Marlin can't leave Dory out of guilt, because he thinks he's the only thing keeping her together.

00:12:12.180 --> 00:12:15.240
And that is the true story of Finding Nemo:

00:12:15.240 --> 00:12:20.800
how a con artist manipulated the emotions
of a grieving father into letting her into his home.

00:12:20.940 --> 00:12:24.240
But hey, that's just a theory...

00:12:24.240 --> 00:12:26.240
a FILM THEORY!

00:12:26.440 --> 00:12:28.340
Aaaaand... CUT!

